{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj5C9cSkVV2FLeLjfKT6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I couldn't look at anything. This session is in don't-ask mode, so the `hive-debug-cli` skill and file search were both blocked. What follows comes from general knowledge of Hive CLI, not from your setup.\n\n**Likely cause:** A hang followed by a bare `command failed` usually means the CLI couldn't reach the Hive registry. The request waits until it times out, and the error that comes back has no Hive error code. When the registry does answer, you normally get a coded error like 103 or 301 instead.\n\nCommon reasons, roughly most to least likely:\n\n1. **Network, DNS or proxy trouble.** You're on WSL2, which often has DNS problems after sleep or a VPN change. Check with:\n   ```bash\n   curl -v https://app.graphql-hive.com/graphql\n   ```\n   If that also hangs, the problem is your network, not Hive. Things to try:\n   - Run `wsl --shutdown` from Windows and reopen WSL.\n   - Check `/etc/resolv.conf`.\n   - If you're behind a corporate proxy, set `HTTPS_PROXY`.\n2. **Wrong registry endpoint.** If you use self-hosted Hive, check `--registry.endpoint`, `HIVE_REGISTRY` or `hive.json`. A stale or unreachable URL behaves exactly like this.\n3. **Hive itself is having problems.** Check status.graphql-hive.com.\n4. **Old CLI version.** Older versions swallowed errors more often. Try `npx @graphql-hive/cli@latest <same command>`.\n\n**To get more detail:**\n- Rerun with `--debug` if your version supports it.\n- Rerun with `DEBUG=* hive <command>` to see the HTTP traffic.\n- Check `echo $HIVE_TOKEN` (or your `--registry.accessToken` value) to make sure the right token is being picked up.\n\nIf you paste the exact command and any output from a `--debug` run, I can narrow it down. Or allow the `hive-debug-cli` skill and I'll go through its error-code checks."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null